<div class="page">

  <div class="series-label">Likutay Halachos &mdash; Yoreh Day&lsquo;ah</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Gairim <span style="font-weight:normal;font-size:17px;">(Laws of Converts)</span></h1>
  <h2>Halachah 3</h2>
  <h3>Inyan Giyur, Mishpat, v&rsquo;Binyan Bais ha-Mikdash &mdash; The Matter of Conversion, Judgment, and the Building of the Holy Temple</h3>
  <hr class="rule">

  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <div class="section-num">1</div>
  <p>
    According to the Torah-teaching that begins: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;One who
    continually strives to draw human beings to the service of the Blessed One&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="editorial-note">((Siman 59 in Likutay Moharan, Part One))</span>, see there.
    And it is explained there that one who strives to draw people close to the Blessed One must
    guard himself so that the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> [husks] and the evil
    of those people not take hold of him. For this person who strives to draw close and to
    &ldquo;make souls,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; he is building the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> [the Holy Temple]. And the remedy against this
    &mdash; to be saved from the evil of those people he is bringing close, so that it not take
    hold of his heart &mdash; is that he summon the angels of his heart, which is the aspect
    of the <span class="key-term">hislahavus ha-lev</span> [the blazing of the heart]. This is
    accomplished through the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> [judgment] &mdash;
    that a person judges himself &mdash; which is the aspect of fire, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;by fire of God shall judgment be rendered&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 66:16]</span>. And this fire burns the evil and the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> so that they not take hold of the heart of the
    worthy person who draws souls close to the Blessed One. And then the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> &mdash; namely, the souls he draws close
    &mdash; also receives the power of the fire, which burns the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>
    from taking hold of them. And then the judgment goes out to light; that is, the heart
    blazes and sits upon the throne, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He has established a throne for judgment&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 9:8]</span>. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the faithful city, full of justice&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 1:21]</span> &mdash; &ldquo;city&rdquo; being the
    aspect of Yerushalayim, the Holy City, namely the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, which clothes the judgment &mdash; and
    this is the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;at that time they shall call
    Yerushalayim the Throne of God, and all the nations shall gather to it in the name of
    God&hellip; and they shall no longer walk after the stubbornness of their evil heart&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yirmiyahu 3:17]</span> &mdash; meaning, even though many
    nations will draw close to the Blessed One, the evil will nonetheless not be able to sway
    their hearts, God forbid. And all this is accomplished through the fire of the angels of
    the heart, as above. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;My Shabbosos shall you observe&rdquo;</em></span>
    &mdash; this is <span class="aramaic">igulah v&rsquo;ribua d&rsquo;ligo</span> [the inner
    circle and square], etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mem</span> and
    <span class="key-term">samech</span> in the Tablets. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;I will give them, in My house and within My walls, a
    hand and a name&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 56:5]</span>.
    And this is the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;house and wealth are the
    inheritance of fathers&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Mishlai 19:14]</span>
    &mdash; see there the full matter well.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 2 -->
  <div class="section-num">2</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">gairim</span> who require milah and
    afterwards immersion. And it is also impossible to convert <span class="key-term">gairim</span>
    except through a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> of three &mdash; as our Sages derived
    from the word <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> [judgment] written in connection with the
    <span class="key-term">ger</span>:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;one law shall there be for you and for the
    <span class="key-term">ger</span>&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Bamidbar 15:16]</span>.
    For the <span class="key-term">ger</span>, having been exceedingly far from holiness &mdash;
    utterly distant &mdash; and wishing to emerge from the impurity and filth of the gentile
    world to the holiness of Israel, it is therefore impossible to bring him close to holiness
    except through a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> of three, where the primary
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> resides. And therefore specifically the
    <span class="key-term">bais din</span> has the power to bring him close and to take him
    under the wings of the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span> &mdash; for since they are a
    <span class="key-term">bais din</span> of three who have the power to execute judgment, and
    all adjudication is done in a court of three, their power of judgment is such that the evil
    of the <span class="key-term">gairim</span> cannot take hold of their hearts, for the fire
    of their judgment burns that evil, as above. And therefore the word
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is written in connection with the
    <span class="key-term">ger</span> &mdash; to teach that the <span class="key-term">ger</span>
    requires a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> of three who are masters of judgment.
    For it is impossible for the <span class="key-term">ger</span> to draw close to the Blessed
    One except through the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as explained in the
    teaching above &mdash; in order to arouse the fire of judgment to burn the evil that had
    taken hold of him, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <div class="section-num">3</div>
  <p>
    And therefore the <span class="key-term">ger</span> first requires milah. For the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> is the evil, the <span class="key-term">zuhamas
    ha-nachash</span>, that had taken hold of him &mdash; and it is impossible to bring him close
    unless he is first circumcised, removing the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> from him,
    which is the evil that had gripped him. Only afterwards can he be brought close to the
    service of the Blessed One. For the primary work is to first strive to subdue and nullify
    the evil gripping him &mdash; and therefore if the immersion preceded the milah, it is
    of no account, for first the evil must be removed, which is the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span>. And afterwards he may be purified through immersion
    and brought close to the Blessed One. For the primary task is to remove and burn the evil
    that had taken hold of those souls who wish to draw close &mdash; for originally he was among
    the nations and the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;this is Yerushalayim; I have set her in the midst of
    the nations&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yechezkel 5:5]</span>. Therefore
    the evil must first be removed from the <span class="key-term">gairim</span> through milah,
    and afterwards he is purified through immersion and brought close to the Blessed One.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <div class="section-num">4</div>
  <p>
    For milah and immersion are in the aspect of <span class="key-term">mem</span> and
    <span class="key-term">samech</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span> [circle and square] explained in the
    teaching above, which are the aspects of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> and
    <span class="key-term">kisai</span> [throne] explained there. For the mitzvah of milah,
    which is the nullification of the evil that is the <span class="key-term">orlah</span>,
    this is in the aspect of nullification of the forbidden within sixty, which is through the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">samech</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> [kingship], the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;sixty are the queens&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Shir ha-Shirim 6:8]</span>. And
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is in the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">cherev pifiyos</span> [double-edged sword], as is cited elsewhere
    &mdash; the aspect of the <span class="key-term">cherev nokemes nikam bris</span>
    [the sword that avenges the vengeance of the covenant], which is the aspect of the sword
    and knife of the milah that cuts the <span class="key-term">orlah</span> and nullifies the
    evil. The power of this sword is drawn from the aspect of the fire of judgment above, in
    the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for by fire shall God render judgment, and by His
    sword against all flesh&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 66:16]</span>.
    And this is the aspect of the verses customarily recited on a day when there is a milah:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;exaltations of God in their throat and a double-edged
    sword in their hand&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 149:6]</span>
    &mdash; namely the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> above, which is the
    aspect of &ldquo;exaltations of God in their throat,&rdquo; the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus peh</span> [the kingship of the mouth], which is the aspect
    of the double-edged sword that subdues, cuts, and nullifies the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> &mdash; the blemishing of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>
    &mdash; in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">cherev nokemes nikam bris</span>. And
    the verse concludes: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;to execute vengeance among the
    nations&hellip; to execute upon them the judgment written&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash;
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> specifically, for the sword above executes upon the
    nations the <span class="key-term">mishpat kasuv</span> [the written judgment], for the
    sword is the aspect of the power of fire drawn from the aspect of judgment, which cuts and
    burns the evil that is the <span class="key-term">orlah</span>. It is thus found that the
    milah is the aspect of nullifying the evil through the fire of judgment, which is the sword
    and knife of the milah &mdash; which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>,
    the aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span> [throne of judgment]. And
    then the judgment goes out to light, and sits upon the throne. And then one merits the
    holiness of Shabbos, which is the aspect of
    <span class="aramaic">igulah v&rsquo;ribua d&rsquo;ligo</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> and <span class="key-term">samech</span>, as explained in
    the teaching above. And this is the aspect of the immersion after the milah &mdash; this
    being the aspect of the <span class="key-term">mem</span>, the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">ribua</span> above &mdash; for the immersion is in a
    <span class="key-term">mikveh</span> of forty <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;ah</span>,
    that is, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mem</span> above. And then the one who comes
    to be purified is hidden within the forty <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;ah</span> of the
    <span class="key-term">mikveh</span> and becomes a throne and chariot for the
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> above, which is the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span> above. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;at that time they shall call Yerushalayim the Throne
    of God, and all the nations shall gather to it&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash;
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;gather&rdquo;</em></span>
    (<span class="aramaic">v&rsquo;nikvu</span>) precisely, in the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">mikveh</span> of forty <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;ah</span>
    above, which is <span class="key-term">mem</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above &mdash; through which all the nations draw
    close and convert, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <div class="section-num">5</div>
  <p>
    It is thus found that it is impossible to draw close <span class="key-term">baalai
    teshuvah</span> [penitents] and <span class="key-term">gairim</span> to the Blessed One
    except through the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. For this
    reason people travel to <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> on <span class="key-term">Rosh
    Hashanah</span> &mdash; <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span> specifically. For
    anyone who truly wishes to draw close to the Blessed One in earnest cannot do so except
    through the true <span class="key-term">tzaddik</span> of the generation. And the
    <span class="key-term">tzaddik</span> can only bring him close through the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. And therefore they travel specifically on
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>, for <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>
    is the day of judgment. And therefore it is the first day of the Ten Days of Repentance,
    and on it people travel to <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> to draw close to the
    Blessed One, for then the judgment goes out to light and burns the evil of those who wish
    to draw close, as above. And then the judgment sits upon its throne, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He has established a throne for judgment&rdquo;</em></span>,
    as above. For all of this is accomplished specifically on <span class="key-term">Rosh
    Hashanah</span>, which is the day of judgment. And therefore then is the primary time for
    drawing close to the Blessed One through the true <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span>
    of the generation. For all of our service on <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>
    is to reveal His blessed Divinity to all who come into the world, until the distant ones
    hear and come, and all of them convert and draw close to the Blessed One &mdash; as we say
    in all the prayers of <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span> and <span class="key-term">Yom
    Kippur</span>: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Place now Your fear, O God our Lord,
    upon all Your works, and Your dread upon all that You have created&hellip; and they shall
    all form a single union to do Your will&hellip; and You, God, shall reign alone over all
    Your works,&rdquo;</em></span> etc.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <div class="section-num">6</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>. For the voice of the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which goes and grows exceedingly strong, is the aspect
    of awe (<span class="key-term">yirah</span>), in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;if a shofar is sounded in a city, will the people not
    tremble?&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Amos 3:6]</span>. And
    <span class="key-term">yirah</span> is in the aspect of fire, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for God your Lord is a consuming fire, a great and
    awesome God&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 4:24]</span>. And
    therefore the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> [divine powers] and
    <span class="key-term">yirah</span> and <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, arouses
    thereby the fire of judgment above, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the voice of God hews flames of fire&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 29:7]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> of <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>
    is the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> of a ram &mdash; in the aspect of the ram of
    Yitzchak, which is in the aspect of the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>
    at the giving of the Torah. And as we recall the voices of the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> at the giving of the Torah on
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;You revealed Yourself&hellip; and the voice of the
    shofar&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. And the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>
    at the giving of the Torah went forth with fire and torches, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and all the people saw the thunderings and the
    torches&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Sh&rsquo;mos 20:15]</span>, and as
    stated in many verses. For at the giving of the Torah all of Israel were in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> &mdash; for then was the beginning of their drawing
    close to their Father in Heaven, having emerged from the impurity of Egypt to the holiness
    of Israel, as Rashi explains there. And therefore we read the Book of Rus on
    <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span>, for <span class="key-term">Shavuos</span>, which is
    the giving of the Torah, is the aspect of the drawing close of
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span>.
    And because of this there was at the giving of the Torah: a voice of a
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, fire, and torches &mdash; all of which is the aspect
    of the fire of judgment above &mdash; in order to burn the evil that had taken hold of them
    from the impurity of Egypt. And therefore the Torah was given through fire, which is the
    aspect of the fire of judgment above, for the Torah itself is called
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>. And because at the giving of the Torah the fire of
    judgment was fully aroused, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and His words we heard from within the fire&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 5:21]</span> &mdash; therefore in truth they were then
    entirely purified from the grip of evil, as they said:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;engraved upon the tablets&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash;
    read not <span class="aramaic">charut</span> [engraved] but <span class="aramaic">chayrus</span>
    [freedom] &mdash; freedom from the Angel of Death <span class="verse-source">((Eruvin 54)</span>.
    For the <span class="key-term">zuhamas ha-nachash</span> was then entirely nullified &mdash;
    for <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;their filth ceased at the giving of the Torah&rdquo;</em></span>
    &mdash; through the aspect of the fire of judgment above, aroused through the voice of the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> that has the power to arouse the fire of judgment, in
    the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the voice of God hews flames of
    fire&rdquo;</em></span>. And the primary corruption that occurred afterwards through the sin
    of the golden calf came only through the <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span>
    [mixed multitude] &mdash; who are in the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> that it is forbidden to convert, whose evil the
    judgment cannot subdue &mdash; as is well explained at the end of the teaching above.
    And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">mem</span> and
    <span class="key-term">samech</span> in the Tablets mentioned in the teaching above, for
    the Tablets, which are the Torah through which their evil was subdued and their filth
    ceased, being given from within the fire as above, are themselves in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> and <span class="key-term">samech</span> &mdash; the
    <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span> above, which is the aspect of nullifying
    the evil, as above. And at the beginning, when the Blessed One revealed Himself to Moshe
    Rabbeinu to redeem Israel from Egypt, the verse states:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and an angel of God appeared to him in a flame of fire
    from within the bush&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Sh&rsquo;mos 3:2]</span>
    &mdash; for since the Blessed One commanded him to take Israel out of the impurity of Egypt
    and bring them close to the Blessed One, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span>,
    the fire of the angels of the heart was immediately aroused &mdash; for only through this
    could Israel be brought close to the Blessed One, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <div class="section-num">7</div>
  <p>
    It is thus found that the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> arouses the
    fire of judgment above, through which the evil is subdued &mdash; and specifically through
    this are <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and the distant ones able to be brought close
    to the Blessed One. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Sound the shofar at the New Moon, at the appointed
    time for our festival day, for it is a statute for Israel, a judgment for the God of
    Yaakov&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 81:4&ndash;5]</span> &mdash;
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> specifically, for we must sound the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> in order to arouse through it the judgment above, as
    above. And this is why we recite before the shofar-blowing the entire 47th Psalm, which
    speaks entirely of the drawing close of all the nations to the Blessed One &mdash; that
    they shall all convert and return to the Blessed One, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;All you nations, clap hands&hellip; for God is Most
    High, awesome, a great King over&rdquo;</em></span> all, etc. &mdash; the aspect of awe,
    which is the aspect of judgment. And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;a great
    King over all the earth&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; for all shall convert and return to Him.
    And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He subdues peoples beneath us&rdquo;</em></span>,
    etc. And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He chooses our inheritance for
    us&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; this inheritance is Yerushalayim, for through converting
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, Yerushalayim, the Holy City, is built &mdash; which
    is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> built through the
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, as explained in the teaching above. And this:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the pride of Yaakov&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash;
    <span class="key-term">Yaakov</span> specifically, for Yaakov is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;judgment
    for the God of Yaakov&rdquo;</em></span>. And all of this is aroused through the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span>. And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;God
    ascends with the teruah; God with the voice of the shofar&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; for
    through this the judgment is aroused, as above. And this is why the Psalm concludes:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the nobles of the nations are gathered, the people
    of the God of Avraham&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; namely,
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, for Avraham was the head of the
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, as our Sages said on this verse. For through the
    voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, <span class="key-term">gairim</span> are made.
    Therefore we recite this entire Psalm, which speaks of <span class="key-term">gairim</span>,
    before the sounding of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> &mdash; for the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> gathers all the scattered and lost, who are in the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">gairim</span> &mdash; that is, all who are distant and
    scattered from the Blessed One are all brought close through the voice of the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. As we say on
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Sound the great shofar for our freedom, and lift a
    banner to gather our exiles, and bring near our dispersed from among the nations&rdquo;</em></span>,
    etc. And as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;on that day a great shofar shall be blown, and those
    lost in the land of Assyria and those scattered in the land of Egypt shall come and bow
    before God on the holy mountain in Yerushalayim&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 27:13]</span>. For the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> gathers and brings close the lost and scattered, who
    are the distant ones from holiness, drawing them close to the Blessed One through the fire
    of judgment, which is the aspect of the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>.
    And through this the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, which is the aspect
    of Yerushalayim, is built. And this:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and they shall bow before God on the holy mountain
    in Yerushalayim&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the holy
    mountain in Yerushalayim&rdquo;</em></span> specifically, namely the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> above, which is built through the lost and
    scattered who come to bow before God and to draw close to His service. And all of this
    through the voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">yirah</span> and the fire of judgment above. And therefore there was
    a <span class="key-term">shofar</span> at the <span class="key-term">bais din</span>,
    for the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <div class="section-num">8</div>
  <p>
    And therefore all the voices of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> are threefold
    &mdash; as they said: the measure of a <span class="key-term">tekiah</span> is like three
    <span class="key-term">teruos</span>, the measure of a <span class="key-term">teruah</span>
    is like three <span class="key-term">sh&rsquo;varim</span>, etc. This is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, which is threefold, for judgment is in a court of
    three. And therefore the primary <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is the aspect of
    Yaakov, the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;judgment for the God of
    Yaakov&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; for Yaakov is the third of the Patriarchs, in the aspect
    of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Yaakov, the rope of His inheritance&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 32:9]</span>, as is known: like a rope that grows from
    three strands. For Yaakov comprises all three Patriarchs, who are the aspect of a court of
    three from whom the judgment emerges &mdash; through which <span class="key-term">gairim</span>
    are converted in a court of three who draw their power from the three Patriarchs. And the
    primary one is Yaakov, whose bed was complete without any blemish. For as long as Yaakov,
    the third of the Patriarchs and the decisive one, had not yet come, it was impossible to
    entirely expel the evil. And therefore Avraham and Yitzchak, who would convert
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, were not entirely free from the grip of evil. And
    therefore from Avraham came Yishmael, and from Yitzchak came Esav &mdash; for the judgment
    had not yet gone out in its fullness to subdue and entirely nullify the evil, until Yaakov
    the third came, in whom the fullness of judgment was complete, and then the evil was entirely
    subdued through the judgment in its fullness. And therefore his bed was complete, with no
    grip of evil and no disqualification at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore the primary aspect of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> is the
    <span class="key-term">teruah</span>, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;a day of teruah shall it be for you&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Bamidbar 29:1]</span>. And as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;God ascends with the teruah&rdquo;</em></span>. For
    the <span class="key-term">teruah</span> is threefold in voices, and it is the aspect of
    Yaakov, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He has not looked at iniquity in Yaakov, nor seen
    perversity in Israel; God his Lord is with him, and the <span class="key-term">teruah</span>
    of the King is within him&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Bamidbar 23:21]</span>
    &mdash; for through the <span class="key-term">teruah</span>, which is the aspect of Yaakov,
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, He has not looked at iniquity in
    Yaakov, nor seen perversity in Israel &mdash; that is, no evil is found in them, for all
    the evil is nullified through the fire of judgment, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <div class="section-num">9</div>
  <p>
    And this is why on the third day of creation &mdash; which is the aspect of Yaakov, as is
    known &mdash; we recite Psalm 82, which speaks of judgment, which is also in the three
    aspects of Yaakov as above, as it is written: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;God
    stands in the divine assembly&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. And this is the conclusion of that
    Psalm: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Rise, O God, judge the earth, for You shall
    inherit all the nations&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 82:8]</span>
    &mdash; for through the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, all nations draw close to the
    Blessed One. And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for You shall inherit&rdquo;</em></span>
    &mdash; <span class="key-term">tinchal</span> [inherit] precisely, for this inheritance is
    Yerushalayim the Holy City, which is the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>
    built through the <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 10 -->
  <div class="section-num">10</div>
  <p>
    And this is why we say on <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for Your judgments they stood today, for all are
    Your servants&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 119:91]</span>
    &mdash; for through <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> all draw close to the Blessed One,
    and then <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;all are Your servants,&rdquo;</em></span>
    for all of them come to serve Him.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 11 -->
  <div class="section-num">11</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of what we say to the <span class="key-term">ger</span> who comes to
    convert: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;What have you seen that you come to
    convert? Do you not know that Israel in this time are afflicted and oppressed&rdquo;</em></span>,
    etc. For at the outset it is necessary to push him away somewhat. And by disparaging him
    and pushing him away a little &mdash; hinting to him that he is not worthy of drawing close
    to us &mdash; and then, when he is willing nonetheless and says, &ldquo;I know and I am not
    worthy,&rdquo; by this the evil within him is subdued and separated from him. In the aspect
    of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and Orpah kissed her mother-in-law; but Rus clung
    to her&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Rus 1:14]</span>. Orpah is the aspect
    of the evil that seeks to grip the <span class="key-term">ger tzedek</span>, the aspect of
    Rus &mdash; and this evil is separated through the initial distancing, by which they wished
    to push the <span class="key-term">ger</span> away at first. For this distancing is the
    aspect of the <span class="aramaic">b&rsquo;tisha</span> [striking] &mdash; that they strike
    him &mdash; as cited in the holy Zohar:
    <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;a tree that does not take to the light&hellip; a body that
    does not take to the light of the soul, they strike it&rdquo;</span>. For by wishing to
    repel and distance him, and he forces and compels himself to draw close even though they
    distance him &mdash; by this he strikes and subdues the evil gripping him. And this itself
    is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above &mdash; for the one who
    draws <span class="key-term">gairim</span> or <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span>
    close weighs his words with judgment, beginning by distancing him &mdash; which is the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">din</span> and <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>,
    judging him as unworthy and repelling and showing him distance. And if he is strong in his
    resolve and says &ldquo;even so, and I am unworthy&rdquo; &mdash; he has already accepted
    the judgment and ruling, having stood the test of distancing. And through this the evil
    gripping him is subdued and separated, and then he is worthy, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of what the true <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> sometimes
    do: distancing a certain person at first &mdash; all of this is for that person&rsquo;s
    good as a test, for the distancing is ultimately for the sake of the closest of closeness.
    In the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;peace, peace to the far and to the
    near&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 57:19]</span> &mdash; for it
    is impossible to bring the distant close except by having them stand the test of distancing,
    through which their evil is subdued, as above. For the distancing by the true
    <span class="key-term">tzaddik</span> is itself the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    above &mdash; that he weighs his words with judgment and knows how to speak with each and
    every person: to bring this one close and to distance that one &mdash; all according to the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> in his heart. And through this he examines, tests,
    and probes them as to whether they come to draw close with a complete heart. And when they
    stand the test and force themselves to draw close even though they are distanced greatly
    &mdash; this is the aspect of the <span class="aramaic">b&rsquo;tisha</span> above, that
    they strike their own body thereby and break all the obstacles, both from without and from
    within. For through breaking all these obstacles and trials, the evil gripping them is
    thereby subdued, and precisely through this they truly draw close to the Blessed One.
  </p>
  <p>
    Furthermore, through the true <span class="key-term">tzaddik</span> weighing his words with
    judgment and initially distancing them, he is thereby able to avoid bringing close the
    wicked ones who are unworthy of being brought close, as is explained in the teaching above
    &mdash; for they will certainly not be able to stand the test of distancing, and they will
    truly distance themselves when he pushes them away, and he will be saved from them.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 12 -->
  <div class="section-num">12</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the one hundred voices sounded on
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>. This is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> and <span class="key-term">samech</span> above, which
    add up to one hundred &mdash; for through the voice of the
    <span class="key-term">shofar</span> we arouse the judgment above, through which the fire
    of judgment goes out and burns the evil that has taken hold of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>. All of this is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> and <span class="key-term">samech</span>, the aspect of
    Shabbos, the aspect of <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span>, as explained in
    the teaching above. And therefore we sound one hundred voices, for
    <span class="key-term">mem</span> and <span class="key-term">samech</span> equal one hundred,
    as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 13 -->
  <div class="section-num">13</div>
  <p>
    And therefore we do not sound the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> on Shabbos. For on
    Shabbos there is no need to arouse the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> in order to
    nullify the evil, for on Shabbos the evil is automatically nullified, as above, in the
    aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;My Shabbosos shall you observe &mdash; this
    is the <span class="aramaic">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span>&rdquo;</em></span>, etc., as above.
    And therefore on Shabbos the aspect of <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua
    d&rsquo;ligo</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mem</span> and
    <span class="key-term">samech</span>, is automatically rectified, and there is no need to
    arouse the one hundred voices of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 14 -->
  <div class="section-num">14</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">kumas chatzos</span> [rising at
    midnight] &mdash; when one stirs from sleep and musters strength to engage in Torah and to
    mourn the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>. For the primary
    destruction of the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> was on account of the
    corruption of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as cited in the words of our Sages:
    the primary destruction of Yerushalayim was because they perverted judgment within it. And
    as it is written: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;those who turn justice to
    wormwood&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. And similarly many verses that speak of the exile of
    Israel and the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> mention
    the corruption of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as the reason Yerushalayim was
    destroyed. For Yerushalayim and the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> are the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, as explained in the teaching
    above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And since the primary building and existence of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> is through the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above, therefore the place of judgment was in
    the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>, which is the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> &mdash; for there in the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> the great Sanhedrin sat in the
    <span class="key-term">Lishkas ha-Gazis</span> [the Chamber of Hewn Stone], from which
    judgment went forth to all the world. For the primary existence of the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>, which is the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, is through
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. For the primary clarification of all the
    holy sparks from within the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> was in the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>, where all the
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> were performed through the
    <span class="key-term">korbanos</span> [sacrifices] and the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;tores</span> [incense] that would atone for all sins
    &mdash; which is the aspect of clarifying the holy sparks, as is known. And therefore
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> was needed there, to arouse the fire
    of judgment to burn the evil so that it not take hold of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, as above. And therefore the place of
    judgment was there, as above, and through the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> that
    was there, the fire above was thereby aroused. And this is the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">eish ha-mizbayach</span> [the fire of the Altar] &mdash; this fire
    burned the evil so that it not take hold of the <span class="key-term">Haichal
    ha-Kodesh</span>, etc., as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 15 -->
  <div class="section-num">15</div>
  <p>
    And this is why our Sages said: why is the section of
    <span class="key-term">Mishpatim</span> juxtaposed to the section of the Altar? To tell
    you to place judges next to the Altar &mdash; as above. For the fire of the Altar is drawn
    through the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above. And therefore the judges
    sat to execute judgment next to the Altar, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 16 -->
  <div class="section-num">16</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of what was inside the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>:
    the Table in the north and the Menorah in the south, and the incense altar
    (<span class="key-term">mizbayach m&rsquo;kater k&rsquo;tores</span>) between the Table
    and the Menorah. For the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;tores</span> [incense] is in the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">gairim</span> &mdash; for the primary clarification of the
    holy sparks from within the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> was accomplished
    through the eleven spices of the incense, as is known, which is the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>: drawing them out from the depths of the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> and bringing them close to holiness. And the
    primary <span class="key-term">gairim</span> are made through the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> [hand and name] above, which are the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span>, as explained in the teaching above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the Table in the north and the Menorah in the south &mdash; the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> above.
    <span class="key-term">&ldquo;Yad&rdquo;</span> [hand] is the aspect of wisdom that flows
    from the House of God, through which one has the power to weigh one&rsquo;s words in
    judgment and to raise up many students, as explained in the teaching above. And this is the
    aspect of the Menorah in the south, which is the aspect of wisdom &mdash; for
    <span class="aramaic">m&rsquo;narata da raisha</span> [the candelabra is the head], for
    the oil of the Menorah is the aspect of wisdom and knowledge, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and oil on your head shall not be lacking&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Koheles 9:8]</span>. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, which is the aspect of wisdom, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the wisdom of God was within him to render
    judgment&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Melachim I 3:28]</span>. The Table
    in the north is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> [name] above, which is
    the aspect of wealth &mdash; the aspect of the table of kings, the aspect of sustenance and
    wealth. And this is the Table in the north, for from there wealth flows, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;from the north gold comes&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Iyov 37:22]</span>. And through all these aspects &mdash; Table
    and Menorah, which are the aspects of <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> above
    &mdash; one makes <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> and
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, which are the aspect of clarifying the holy sparks
    from within the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;tores</span>, as above. And therefore the incense altar stood
    between the Table and the Menorah, as above. It is thus found that the primary existence of
    Yerushalayim and the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> [Holy Temple] is through
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above, and the primary destruction of
    Yerushalayim was through the corruption of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above.
    And in the future, the primary building of Yerushalayim shall be through
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Tzion shall be redeemed through mishpat&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 1:27]</span> &mdash; for through
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> are
    made, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 17 -->
  <div class="section-num">17</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">kumas chatzos</span>. For awakening from
    sleep is the aspect of the return of the <span class="key-term">mochin</span>, for sleep is
    the departure of the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> and waking from sleep is their
    return. And when the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> return, the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> dependent upon the <span class="key-term">moach</span>
    and wisdom is aroused, as above. And this is accomplished at midnight, for then is the time
    of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> that is the aspect of awakening from sleep, in the
    aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Awake and rouse Yourself for my
    judgment&rdquo;</em></span>, and in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;at midnight I rise&hellip; for Your righteous
    judgments&rdquo;</em></span>, as above. And therefore one rises at midnight and mourns the
    destruction of the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> and engages in Torah
    &mdash; for the primary destruction of Yerushalayim and the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> was through the corruption of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, and the primary building of the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> is through <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>,
    as above. And therefore at midnight, when the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is
    aroused as above, one engages in Torah, for the Torah is called
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, for the primary existence of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> is through
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. And all of this is done to arouse and
    awaken the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> until the light of day. And then at
    daybreak the judgment goes out to light and sits upon the throne, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;House of David, judge judgment in the morning&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yirmiyahu 21:12]</span> &mdash;
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;House of David&rdquo;</em></span> specifically, for
    the House of David is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span> above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 18 -->
  <div class="section-num">18</div>
  <p>
    For every person comes into this world for no other purpose than to perform
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> [clarifications] &mdash; to clarify holy sparks from
    the depths of the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> that fell there through the
    sin of Adam ha-Rishon, or through his own sins in a previous gilgul. And the clarification
    of the sparks is the aspect of <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and
    <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span>, as above. And therefore a person must guard
    himself greatly and judge himself constantly in order to arouse the fire of judgment above
    &mdash; to subdue the evil gripping those sparks he must clarify, so that it not take hold
    of him, God forbid. For this reason a person is truly in great danger in this world, and
    must judge himself greatly every single day, think through his ways, and arouse his heart
    to the Blessed One &mdash; which is the matter of <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span>
    [private personal prayer] and intimate conversation between oneself and one&rsquo;s Creator,
    about which our Rabbeinu, may his memory be for a blessing, warned us countless times and
    as mentioned many times in his holy works. For in proportion to the great danger that every
    person faces in this world &mdash; great and small alike &mdash; from the evil gripping the
    sparks he must clarify, one must be extremely vigilant in judging oneself daily. For even
    the lowliest of Israel also performs <span class="key-term">birurim</span> according to his
    own level. And because these holy sparks he must clarify are intermixed with evil, great
    dangers therefore face him lest the evil take hold of him, God forbid. And therefore our
    Sages said: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;every day a person&rsquo;s
    <span class="key-term">yetzer ha-ra</span> [evil inclination] overpowers him, and were it
    not for the Holy Blessed One helping him&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. And therefore there is no
    wisdom, understanding, or counsel to stand against them except through
    <span class="key-term">hisbodedus</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> mentioned in the teaching above &mdash; that a person
    must judge himself: that is, to seclude himself with his Creator and to pour out his
    conversation before Him, and to examine and judge himself in all his ways, and to rebuke
    and admonish himself every single day &mdash; in order thereby to arouse the blazing of
    his heart &mdash; in order that the fire of judgment be aroused &mdash; in order to burn
    and subdue the evil so that it not take hold of his heart, God forbid, as above. And
    therefore our Sages said:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Do not trust in yourself until the day of your
    death&rdquo;</em></span>. And as we have already seen how many
    <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> and great people, and how many worthy and
    distinguished persons, who had already engaged in the service of God in earnest for days
    and years &mdash; and afterwards were caught in severe transgressions, God forbid,
    <span class="aramaic">Rachmana litzlan</span> &mdash; as is cited in the writings of the
    Ari regarding this matter. But all of it is in accordance with the above &mdash; for since
    every person engages in this world only to perform
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span>, and particularly those who engage more intensely in
    the service of God &mdash; they perform new <span class="key-term">birurim</span> every
    single day. And therefore their <span class="key-term">yetzer ha-ra</span> overpowers them
    more and more each day, for they have a new <span class="key-term">yetzer ha-ra</span>
    every day. For every day the evil stirs against them anew &mdash; namely the evil of those
    sparks that have now come to them to be clarified. And therefore sometimes they come to
    certain sparks that lie in the depths of the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>,
    and since they are engaged in clarifying them, the evil thereby takes very strong hold of
    them, God forbid &mdash; <span class="aramaic">Rachmana litzlan</span> &mdash; and they
    can therefore stumble suddenly, God forbid. And therefore one must be extremely, extremely
    vigilant to judge oneself every single day and to think through one&rsquo;s ways, in order
    thereby to arouse the blazing of the heart &mdash; so that the fire of judgment be aroused
    &mdash; in order to burn the evil above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 19 -->
  <div class="section-num">19</div>
  <p>
    And in particular, every person is obligated and required to also strive to bring others
    close to the Blessed One, to make <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> and
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> &mdash; for this is what you were created for, for
    this is the primary <span class="key-term">yishuv ha-olam</span> [settlement of the world].
    As it is written: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He did not create it as a void; He
    formed it to be settled&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 45:18]</span>
    &mdash; that the world be populated by human beings who are people of knowledge, engaged in
    the service of God. For one who does not engage in the service of God is merely an animal
    in human form, as cited in the words of our Rabbeinu many times. And as our Sages warned
    greatly about this, and as is cited in the holy Zohar:
    <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;praiseworthy is the one who takes the hand of the wicked&rdquo;</span>
    &mdash; and it elaborates greatly there on the immensity of his reward, that the Blessed
    One glories in him in all the worlds, see there. And this is the aspect of the mutual
    surety (<span class="key-term">araivus</span>) whereby all of Israel are surety for one
    another &mdash; for every individual of Israel is obligated to speak with his fellow about
    the fear of Heaven every single day. And as we request in many prayers:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;that we merit to learn and to teach, to observe and
    to do and to uphold&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. &mdash; and the primary substance of our
    request is that we merit to teach others paths and counsels in the service of the Blessed
    One. And this: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;to learn and to teach, to observe and
    to do and to uphold&rdquo;</em></span> &mdash; that the learning be only for the sake of
    observing, doing, and upholding, for it is not the study that is primary but the action.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is thus found that every individual of Israel has an obligation to also merit others
    through repentance and to draw souls to the service of the Blessed One &mdash; but one
    must guard oneself greatly from the evil gripping those souls he wishes to bring close.
    And for this one requires <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> &mdash; to judge oneself
    every day &mdash; in order to arouse the fire of judgment to burn the evil so that it not
    take hold of his heart. And also to drive out and burn the evil from the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> &mdash; namely, from those souls he strives
    to bring close, as well explained in the teaching above. And then the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is aroused and the judgment goes out to light, and
    then the judgment sits upon its throne, etc. &mdash; see all of this well and well in the
    teaching above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 20 -->
  <div class="section-num">20</div>
  <p>
    And behold, the primary <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is by day, for judgment is
    not rendered at night except for the conclusion of a verdict, or if the litigants consent.
    And as it is written: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;House of David, judge judgment
    in the morning&rdquo;</em></span>. And therefore <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is
    called <span class="key-term">or</span> [light], as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and Your judgments like a light shall go forth&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 51:4]</span>. For <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    is the aspect of the light of day, for <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is the aspect
    of wisdom and <span class="key-term">mochin</span> as above &mdash; and this is the aspect
    of the light of day, for at night the <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> [knowledge]
    departs, as is known.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore the primary <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is by day. And therefore
    the primary revelation of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>, through which one can
    weigh one&rsquo;s words in judgment, is primarily by day. And when night comes, all the
    guardians of legal jurisdiction arise, etc., and all the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>, etc. And then the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is the aspect of the throne of glory, the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span> above, descends into the
    depths of the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> to perform
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> from there. And because then the
    <span class="key-term">chitzonim</span> [outer forces] greatly overpower, we have no
    strength to burn and subdue them through our own aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>. And therefore nighttime is the time of sleep, which
    is the departure of the <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span>, the departure of the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> &mdash; that is, we remove the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> from ourselves, for the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> has no power to subdue them. Rather, we surrender the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the Blessed One &mdash; that He Himself execute
    the written judgment upon them. And this is the aspect of what we say before sleep:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;into Your hand I entrust my spirit&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 31:6]</span> &mdash; <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;into
    Your hand&rdquo;</em></span> specifically, for we surrender to Him the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> itself. And <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">yad</span> [hand], in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and my hand grasps judgment&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 32:41]</span> &mdash; so that the Blessed One Himself
    execute judgment upon them, in the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for
    judgment belongs to God&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 1:17]</span>.
    And then the Blessed One Himself draws the power of judgment to the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Haichal
    ha-Kodesh</span> above, the aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai</span> above, which
    is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> above, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">igulah</span> &mdash; and then the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>
    receives the power of judgment and descends with the power of judgment throughout all the
    lower worlds, performing <span class="key-term">birurim</span> from the depths of the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> throughout the entire duration of sleep until
    midnight, for <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is the avenging sword, the aspect of
    the sword of judgment above.
  </p>
  <p>
    For at night the primary <span class="key-term">birur</span> is through the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faith], the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>
    above &mdash; for the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> alone goes and performs
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> at night, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;my soul, I desired You in the night&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 26:9]</span>, etc. And the primary desire of the holy
    soul is for the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for Your name and for Your remembrance is the desire
    of the soul&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[ibid.]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    And therefore, even though there is no <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> at night,
    nonetheless we arouse at night the power of the daytime <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    &mdash; for the conclusion of a verdict can be done at night, and also a person can always
    judge himself, for regarding oneself it is certainly as if one had accepted the matter. And
    therefore one confesses before sleep in all the confessions, as cited in the
    <span class="key-term">Ra&rsquo;aya Mehaimna</span> that one must confess all confessions
    before sleep &mdash; and accepts upon oneself the four capital punishments of the court
    &mdash; in order to arouse the power of daytime judgment and to surrender all of this power
    to the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">kisai</span> above, etc. So that it can descend and ascend from the
    depths of the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>. For at night the primary
    <span class="key-term">birur</span> is through the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>,
    which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> as above. For
    <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> above &mdash;
    <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span> above &mdash; are the aspects of day
    and night: <span class="key-term">yad</span>, which is the aspect of wisdom and
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, is the aspect of the light of day, as above; and
    <span class="key-term">shem</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">nefesh</span>
    [soul], is the aspect of night, the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;my soul, I desired You in the night&rdquo;</em></span>,
    as above. For the <span class="key-term">tzaddik</span> performs
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> and makes <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and
    <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> through these two aspects of
    <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> &mdash; for many souls come to him and attach
    themselves to him merely because of his name alone, and afterwards when they come to him,
    he teaches them his wisdom and weighs his words with judgment for each and every individual.
    And the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> is the aspect of sleep, the departure
    of <span class="key-term">da&rsquo;as</span> &mdash; that is, he is not then engaged in
    speaking with them to teach them judgment, to draw his knowledge into them, but rather his
    name alone goes and spreads throughout the world, and through that alone they attach
    themselves to him.
  </p>
  <p>
    And at night, when it is the primary time of <span class="key-term">birur</span> through
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> and <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>
    as above, we then surrender the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the Blessed One
    &mdash; for then all the guardians of legal jurisdiction arise, and we do not know how to
    conduct ourselves with them, for there is evil against which the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> does not avail, as explained at the end of the
    teaching above. And therefore we then surrender the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    to the Blessed One in the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;into Your hand I
    entrust my spirit&rdquo;</em></span>, as above &mdash; so that the Blessed One Himself
    execute judgment upon them. And the Blessed One Himself then draws the power of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>,
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span> above, to rescue it from the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> from the side of death, which is the aspect of
    sleep &mdash; which is one-sixtieth of death &mdash;
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;one-sixtieth&rdquo;</em></span> precisely. And
    therefore before sleep we arouse the sixty mighty ones, all grasping swords, etc. And we
    also recite the sixty letters of <span class="key-term">Birchas Kohanim</span> [the Priestly
    Blessing] &mdash; to guard and rescue the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>,
    which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">igulah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">samech</span>,
    as explained in the teaching above &mdash; in order to rescue the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">samech</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;sixty are the queens&rdquo;</em></span>, from the
    sleep which is one-sixtieth of death. And this is why we say before sleep:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;exaltations of God in their throat and a double-edged
    sword in their hand, to execute vengeance among the nations, reproofs among the
    peoples&hellip; to execute upon them the judgment written&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 149:6&ndash;9]</span>. For then, before sleep, we
    arouse the power of judgment in order to surrender it to the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>,
    which is the aspect of the double-edged sword, the avenging sword, the sword of judgment
    above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 21 -->
  <div class="section-num">21</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of what is stated in the writings of the Ari, that at the time of
    sleep the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> depart from
    <span class="key-term">Z&rsquo;air Anpin</span> upward, and then the
    <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> are drawn to
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> not through <span class="key-term">Z&rsquo;air
    Anpin</span> &mdash; and through this the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> is sawed
    apart and returns face to face. This is the mystery of the
    <span class="key-term">kavanas Rosh Hashanah</span>, the mystery of the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;sirah</span> [sawing], the mystery of sleep and the waking
    from sleep through the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> on
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span> &mdash; see in the
    <span class="key-term">kavanos</span> &mdash; as above. For we surrender the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mochin</span>, to the Blessed One, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for judgment belongs to God&rdquo;</em></span>. And
    then the Blessed One Himself draws the <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> of the
    <span class="key-term">mochin</span> &mdash; the fire of judgment &mdash; to the
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>. And through this it performs
    <span class="key-term">birurim</span> and is fully built, and subdues the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> through the fire of judgment &mdash; and then
    it can return face to face, for it no longer fears the
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span>.
    <span class="editorial-note">((See in the <span class="key-term">kavanas k&rsquo;rias shema
    sheal ha-mitah</span> and the <span class="key-term">kavanos Rosh Hashanah</span>.))</span>
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 22 -->
  <div class="section-num">22</div>
  <p>
    It is thus found that at night, which is the departure of the
    <span class="key-term">mochin</span>, the aspect of sleep, the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> then departs from the person and rises to the Blessed
    One. And the Blessed One Himself draws the power of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which
    is the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>, to guard the person during sleep. And
    therefore in truth great guarding is needed then &mdash; and all of it comes only through
    the power of the daytime <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>: for according to the good
    deeds one performed during the day, correspondingly one merits guarding during sleep, as
    is known. And all good deeds &mdash; doing good and turning from evil &mdash; all of this
    is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;I considered my ways and turned my feet to Your
    testimonies&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 119:59]</span>. And
    when midnight arrives, the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> reaches its ultimate
    diminution and descends very low, and then it cries out in a great voice to be lifted up
    from there &mdash; for with each passing hour the power of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> diminishes during sleep. And therefore anyone who
    wishes to have compassion on the honor of his Master, the honor of His Name, and his own
    soul &mdash; must arouse himself with great arousal precisely at midnight. And through
    waking up then, one thereby arouses the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, in the
    aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Awake and rouse Yourself for my
    judgment,&rdquo;</em></span> and in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;at midnight I rise&hellip; for Your righteous
    judgments&rdquo;</em></span>, as above. And through the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> one raises the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>,
    the aspect of his name and his soul &mdash; and then is the time to build the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> through the power of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>. And therefore one mourns then the destruction of the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>, which was destroyed through the corruption
    of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. And all who mourn for Yerushalayim
    merit to see in its rejoicing and its rebuilding. It is thus found that through mourning
    the destruction of the <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span>, one thereby engages
    in its building &mdash; which is the aspect of building the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> above. And therefore one engages in Torah
    then, for the Torah is called <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, for the primary
    existence of the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> is through
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above. And all of this is done to arouse and
    awaken the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> until the light of day. And then at
    daybreak the judgment goes out to light and sits upon the throne, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;House of David, judge judgment in the morning,&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;House of David&rdquo;</em></span> specifically &mdash;
    for the House of David is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is
    the aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span> above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 23 -->
  <div class="section-num">23</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> and
    <span class="key-term">tefillin</span>, whose time begins from when the day brightens. For
    <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> and <span class="key-term">tefillin</span> are the
    aspects of <span class="key-term">igulah v&rsquo;ribua</span> above &mdash;
    <span class="key-term">yad va-shem</span> above.
    <span class="key-term">Tzitzis</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">igulah</span>,
    the aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai</span> above &mdash; for
    <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">makifim</span>
    [surrounding lights], the aspect of circles, as cited in the words of our Rabbeinu. And
    they are the aspect of <span class="key-term">kisai</span>, for
    <span class="aramaic">t&rsquo;cheles kurs&rsquo;ya d&rsquo;dina</span> [blue is the throne
    of judgment] &mdash; as cited in the <span class="key-term">Tikunim</span>: through
    <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span> one rectifies the throne for the Holy Blessed One.
    <span class="key-term">Tefillin</span> are the aspect of <span class="key-term">ribua</span>
    [square] &mdash; the aspect of tefillin that are square in their stitching and diagonals,
    and the four Scriptural portions &mdash; all of which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">ribua</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    above. For <span class="key-term">tefillin</span> means judgment, as cited: the language of
    <span class="key-term">p&rsquo;lilah</span> [judgment]. For
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mochin</span>
    as above, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tefillin</span>, which are the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">mochin</span>, as is known. And on Shabbos, when the evil
    is nullified as above &mdash; therefore there is no need then to arouse
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> &mdash; and therefore judgment is not rendered on
    Shabbos and <span class="key-term">Yom Tov</span>. And therefore
    <span class="key-term">tefillin</span> are not worn on Shabbos, for then there is no need
    to arouse the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above to nullify the evil &mdash; for
    then the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span> rests, etc., as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 24 -->
  <div class="section-num">24</div>
  <p>
    And therefore before Shabbos, when all the sparks ascend and are gathered into holiness at
    the entry of Shabbos, and there is fear that the evil
    <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> might also cling &mdash; the flame of fire
    therefore descends and burns them, that is, the fire of judgment above. And this is the
    aspect of the hot water bath (<span class="key-term">r&rsquo;chitzas chamin</span>) on
    <span class="key-term">Erev Shabbos</span>, as is known. And afterwards one immerses in the
    <span class="key-term">mikveh</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ribua</span>,
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">mem</span> above, the aspect of the forty
    <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;ah</span> of the <span class="key-term">mikveh</span>.
    For when the fire of judgment descends to burn the evil, afterwards the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> remains only in the aspect of mercy and kindnesses
    (<span class="key-term">rachamim va-chasadim</span>), the aspect of the waters of kindness,
    the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and let justice roll down like
    waters&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Amos 5:24]</span> &mdash; for
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is the <span class="key-term">amuda d&rsquo;emtza&rsquo;isa</span>
    [the middle pillar], comprising both mercy and judgment, fire and water. For this is the
    mystery cited in the <span class="key-term">kavanos</span> above: that initially, before
    the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;sirah</span>, they are back to back, and
    <span class="key-term">Z&rsquo;air Anpin</span> comprises both
    <span class="key-term">chasadim</span> and <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span>.
    And at the time of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;sirah</span>, which occurs through
    sleep every night, and likewise on <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>, all the
    <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> are then drawn to
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, and <span class="key-term">Z&rsquo;air Anpin</span>
    remains as entirely <span class="key-term">chasadim</span> alone &mdash; that is, as above:
    first the fire of judgment is aroused to burn the evil from taking hold of the heart, and
    afterwards all the fire &mdash; the <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> &mdash; is
    drawn to the <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, which is the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, etc., as above. And then the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> remains in the aspect of the waters of kindness alone,
    the aspect of mercy. And this is the aspect of the immersion of the
    <span class="key-term">ger</span> after the milah &mdash; the milah being the aspect of the
    fire of judgment and the double-edged sword, etc., as above, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">kisai</span> above. And after cutting away the evil which is the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> through the double-edged sword above, the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> afterwards remains in the aspect of mercy, the aspect
    of the waters of kindness, as above. And this is the aspect of the immersion in the
    <span class="key-term">mikveh</span> of forty <span class="key-term">s&rsquo;ah</span>,
    as above. And this is the aspect of washing with water or immersing every morning, as
    above. And this is the aspect of what is written in the holy Zohar:
    <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;when night spreads its wings, the gates of Gan Eden are closed
    and the guardians of legal jurisdiction go out into the world, and the world is conducted
    by the hand of the Matronita&rdquo;</span> &mdash; as above. For at night the
    <span class="key-term">mochin</span> depart, which is the aspect of the gates of Gan Eden
    closing, as is known. And this is the departure of the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> upward to the Blessed One. And then the guardians of
    legal jurisdiction go out, as above. And then the world is conducted by the hand of the
    <span class="key-term">Matronita</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">kisai</span>
    above, the aspect of <span class="key-term">shem</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">igulah</span>, the aspect of <span class="key-term">samech</span>,
    the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> above &mdash; for we
    surrender to it the power of judgment to execute the written judgment upon the nations, etc.
    All as above, and understand well.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 25 -->
  <div class="section-num">25</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the immense length of the exile now, the like of which has not
    been since ancient times. For already all the predestined ends have passed, and the matter
    depends only upon <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>. For now the Blessed One desires
    that the redemption be complete, forever, with no exile following it at all &mdash; and
    therefore it is certainly necessary that all of Israel return in
    <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span> and clarify all the <span class="key-term">birurim</span>.
    As our Sages said: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the son of David will not come until
    all the souls in the &lsquo;body&rsquo; (the heavenly treasury) are exhausted&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">((Avodah Zarah 5a))</span>, which is the aspect of clarifying
    all the sparks, as is known. And it is impossible to make <span class="key-term">baalai
    teshuvah</span> and clarify <span class="key-term">birurim</span> except through the aspect
    of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above, which burns the evil &mdash; and then the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> is built spiritually through the souls
    brought close to the Blessed One, and then the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>
    will also be built physically &mdash; namely, the actual building of the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> &mdash; as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and the palace shall stand in its proper place&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yirmiyahu 30:18]</span> &mdash;
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;in its proper place&rdquo;</em></span> specifically,
    namely through the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    But there are wicked ones who are in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span>
    whom it is forbidden to bring close &mdash; for the power of judgment cannot avail against
    them, in the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Your judgments are far above
    them&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 10:5]</span>, as is well
    explained in the teaching above. And therefore most people do not know how to craft counsel
    for themselves regarding the matter of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above &mdash;
    how to bring the distant close, lest they be among the wicked who must not be brought close,
    as above. For certainly no flesh and blood can know this clearly, even the greatest of
    <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span>, and certainly the lesser ones &mdash; for even
    Moshe Rabbeinu, peace be upon him, stumbled in this by bringing close the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span>, who are the wicked that must not be brought close,
    until the Blessed One said to him:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Go down, for your people have acted corruptly&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Sh&rsquo;mos 32:7]</span>, as explained in the teaching above.
    And if so, what shall the lowly ones do? For Moshe Rabbeinu certainly merited the aspect
    of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> in its fullness, having given us the Torah which is
    the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as above, and at the giving of the Torah
    &mdash; when Israel drew close to their Father in Heaven, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span>
    &mdash; he then merited to arouse the angels of the heart, the fire of judgment above, in
    its fullness. And in truth he succeeded, for he merited to subdue the evil entirely, for
    Israel who stood at Mount Sinai &mdash; their filth ceased. For all the evil was entirely
    nullified through the fire of judgment above, as above. And yet afterwards the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span> corrupted things, until the Holy Blessed One said
    &ldquo;Go down,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; because they are in the aspect of the wicked whom it
    is forbidden to bring close, for the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> cannot avail
    against them as above. All the more so ordinary people who certainly cannot rely on their
    own <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> alone, but must judge themselves greatly
    regarding everything in order to arouse the blazing of the heart. And afterwards surrender
    the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the Blessed One in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for judgment belongs to God,&rdquo;</em></span>
    so that the Blessed One Himself give them wisdom and knowledge to weigh their words in
    judgment, and merit to know how to speak with, and how not to speak with, and how to speak,
    and when to speak, and in what manner and what and how much &mdash; for it is impossible for
    a person to calibrate this judgment well as above, particularly now in this exile. And the
    exile is like night, when only the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> must be surrendered
    to the Blessed One as above. For now in this exile, particularly in
    <span class="key-term">ikv&rsquo;ah d&rsquo;meshicha</span> [the footsteps of Mashiach],
    Moshe Rabbeinu himself is compelled to clothe himself within all of Israel and to reincarnate
    with them in order to rectify the blemish he caused in judgment by bringing the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span> close, as cited in the
    <span class="key-term">Tikunim</span> and the holy Zohar in many places. It is thus found
    that now in the power of Moshe Rabbeinu we must rectify the blemish of the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> that was blemished through the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span>. And because of this the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is exceedingly wondrous, hidden, and concealed &mdash;
    for even Moshe Rabbeinu descended from his level through the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span> whom he brought close, as is known. And therefore it
    is said about Israel in this exile: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;she has gone down
    wondrously&rdquo;</em></span>. And the <span class="key-term">ketz</span> [end] is wondrous,
    hidden, and exceedingly concealed, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;how long until the wondrous end?&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Daniel 12:6]</span>. And all of this is because of the wonders
    and the concealment of the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> that must rectify the
    blemish of the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> that occurred through the
    <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span> &mdash; this <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    being wondrous and exceedingly concealed, in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;if a matter of judgment is wondrous for
    you&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Devarim 17:8]</span>. And one must only
    judge oneself well, and afterwards surrender the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to
    the Blessed One alone, in the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for judgment
    belongs to God,&rdquo;</em></span> as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 26 -->
  <div class="section-num">26</div>
  <p>
    For there are two aspects of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>. The first is what a
    person must do: to judge himself every day &mdash; that is, to seclude himself with his
    Creator and to pour out his conversation before Him, and to lay bare all that is in his
    heart before the Blessed One, and to think through his ways and to judge himself in all
    his paths &mdash; whether it is proper for him to spend his days thus, God forbid, in ways
    such as these, etc. And the second aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is to
    weigh one&rsquo;s words with judgment &mdash; that is, to know how to speak and with whom
    to speak and to draw one&rsquo;s wisdom into them, etc. And this is hinted at in the words
    of our Rabbeinu, in the teaching above, where these two aspects are mentioned, as is
    explained there. And the fire of the angel is made from the first aspect: weighing one&rsquo;s
    words with judgment &mdash; and the person judges himself, etc. &mdash; and they are two
    aspects. Weighing one&rsquo;s words, etc., is one aspect; the person judging himself is the
    second aspect. And at the end of the teaching this is explained more clearly, as it is written
    there: for through weighing one&rsquo;s words in judgment, one thereby can teach one&rsquo;s
    wisdom to students, etc. &mdash; see there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now, judging oneself &mdash; this every person can fulfill, as above. But the second aspect
    &mdash; weighing one&rsquo;s words with judgment as above &mdash; this requires great
    assistance from Above, that one merit this knowledge. And furthermore, even afterwards one
    cannot rely on one&rsquo;s own wisdom and judgment alone, for there are wicked ones in the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">Erev Rav</span> &mdash; for whose sake the judgment
    is exalted beyond them &mdash; who are the primary cause of the length of the exile, as is
    known. And therefore one must surrender this <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> to the
    Blessed One &mdash; and the Blessed One knows the hidden things, and will lead him in the
    straight path, so that he merits to calibrate the judgment well: to know with whom to speak,
    etc. And to recognize the wicked who must not be brought close, so that he not bring them
    close at all. And then when the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is rectified, the
    <span class="key-term">Bais ha-Mikdash</span> shall be built, which is the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, as above. And it shall be fulfilled:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;as in the days of your coming out of Egypt, I shall
    show you wonders&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Michah 7:15]</span> &mdash;
    this being the rectification of the wonder of the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>,
    the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;if a matter of judgment is wondrous for
    you,&rdquo;</em></span> through which the <span class="key-term">ketz ha-p&rsquo;laos</span>
    [the wondrous end] is concealed. And may the Blessed One have mercy upon us and show us
    the wonders of the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, and then shall be fulfilled:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Tzion shall be redeemed through mishpat, and her
    returnees through tzedakah&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 1:27]</span>
    &mdash; speedily in our days, Amen and Amen.
    <span class="editorial-note">((Belongs to the above.))</span>
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 27 -->
  <div class="section-num">27</div>
  <p>
    For this reason specifically Yisro merited to give counsel to Moshe Rabbeinu regarding the
    matter of judgment, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;but you shall look out from among all the
    people&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Sh&rsquo;mos 18:21]</span>, etc.
    For Yisro, who is a <span class="key-term">ger tzedek</span>, whose primary drawing close
    to the Blessed One was through <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as above &mdash;
    therefore he merited to add to the Torah the section of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>,
    which is the passage beginning <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and you shall look
    out&rdquo;</em></span>, as above. And therefore the section of the giving of the Torah is
    juxtaposed to the section of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> &mdash; for the fire
    of the giving of the Torah is the aspect of the fire of judgment above, for Israel drew
    close then to the Blessed One.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 28 -->
  <div class="section-num">28</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tekiah-sh&rsquo;varim-teruah</span>, which
    are the aspects of the three Patriarchs, as is cited. For the Patriarchs would convert
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span>, as explained in the teaching above. This is
    accomplished through the <span class="key-term">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">yirah</span> and <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as above,
    as it is written: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;from Heaven You caused judgment to
    be heard; the earth feared and was still&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 76:9]</span>. And this is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Malchuyos</span>, <span class="key-term">Zichronos</span>, and
    <span class="key-term">Shofros</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="key-term">Malchuyos</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">shem</span> above, the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, etc., above. For
    <span class="key-term">shem</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span>
    as above, as we say in <span class="key-term">Malchuyos</span>:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and God shall be King over all the earth; on that day
    God shall be One and His name One&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Z&rsquo;charyah 14:9]</span>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="key-term">Zichronos</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> above, that a person judges himself, through which
    the judgment above is sweetened. For the entire blessing of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;You remember the deeds of the world&rdquo;</em></span>
    speaks of judgment, as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;You remember the deeds of the world and take note&rdquo;</em></span>,
    etc. &mdash; who is not reckoned on this day, etc. &mdash; which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>: that a person must think through his ways, etc.,
    knowing that the Blessed One remembers all and nothing is forgotten before Him, and all his
    deeds shall be remembered before Him on the day of judgment &mdash; and therefore he must
    always judge himself as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="key-term">Shofros</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of the fire of judgment
    above, the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;the voice of God hews flames of
    fire,&rdquo;</em></span> as above, as is cited in this blessing:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and all the people saw the thunderings and the
    torches,&rdquo;</em></span> etc. &mdash; as above. For through all these aspects,
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> and <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> are
    brought close to the Blessed One &mdash; which is the primary aspect of
    <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span>, as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 29 -->
  <div class="section-num">29</div>
  <p>
    And this is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">shamash</span> [sexton] striking with
    a hammer in the morning to wake the world to go to the <span class="key-term">bais
    knesses</span> [synagogue]. For in the morning the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    is aroused, as above &mdash; for then the judgment goes out to light, as above, in the
    aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;judge judgment in the morning,&rdquo;</em></span>
    as above. And this is the aspect of the striking with a hammer &mdash; for striking is the
    aspect of <span class="key-term">din</span> and <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>,
    namely the fire of judgment above, in the aspect of:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Is not My word like fire&hellip; and like a
    hammer&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yirmiyahu 23:29]</span> &mdash; for
    through the fire of judgment in the aspect of striking, all the distant ones are brought
    close to His service. And therefore the world is awoken through this striking to go to the
    <span class="key-term">bais knesses</span>, which is the gathering place of all &mdash;
    that all of them draw close to the Blessed One and go to the
    <span class="key-term">bais knesses</span> to engage in the service of God. For through the
    fire of judgment, the aspect of striking, which burns all the evil from the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> &mdash; through this all of them can draw
    close to the Blessed One and go to the <span class="key-term">bais knesses</span>, which
    is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>. Also, the sound of
    the striking is a strong voice, the aspect of the voice that arouses the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as above &mdash; for the primary arousal of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is through the voice, which is the aspect of the
    voice of the <span class="key-term">shofar</span> as above, for voice is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, as is known. And therefore all the plagues and
    judgments that Moshe executed in Egypt were through striking with the staff, for striking
    is the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> as above. And therefore three strikes
    are made in the morning &mdash; this being the aspect of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    which is in three, as above. And therefore on Shabbos there is no waking for the
    <span class="key-term">bais knesses</span> through striking with a hammer, for on Shabbos
    there is no need to arouse <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>, for on Shabbos the evil
    is nullified, for then the <span class="key-term">Shechinah</span> rests, etc., as above.
  </p>

  <hr class="section-divider">

  <!-- SECTION 30 -->
  <div class="section-num">30</div>
  <p>
    And this is why the blessing <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;restore our judges&rdquo;</em></span>
    is juxtaposed to the blessing <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;who gathers the
    scattered&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. &mdash; for this gathering is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">baalai teshuvah</span> and <span class="key-term">gairim</span>
    who were initially scattered and distant from holiness and are now gathered and drawn close
    to the Blessed One, and all the scattered and dispersed holy sparks ascend. And therefore
    next to this is juxtaposed <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;restore our judges&rdquo;</em></span>
    &mdash; for for this, <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> is needed, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    And this is why after the blessing of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> the blessing
    of the heretics is juxtaposed: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and for the informers
    let there be no hope&rdquo;</em></span>, etc. &mdash; this being the aspect of the wicked
    ones who must not be brought close, against whom the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    does not avail, as above. We request that they have no hope and perish and be uprooted and
    nullified and cause us no harm at all. And then the horn of the
    <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> and the <span class="key-term">chasidim</span>
    shall be exalted &mdash; for once one merits to distance the wicked ones against whom the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> does not avail, then the
    <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> shall be in its proper place, and then the
    <span class="key-term">gairim</span> shall not be able, God forbid, to sway the hearts of
    the <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span> from the Blessed One. And therefore through
    this there shall be a <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;support and security for the
    righteous,&rdquo;</em></span> and then when the <span class="key-term">tzaddikim</span>
    merit the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> in its fullness, the
    <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span> shall be built, as above &mdash; which is
    the aspect of the building of Yerushalayim, as above. And then there shall be the sprouting
    of the horn of David, which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">kisai ha-mishpat</span>
    above, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Malchus</span> as above &mdash; the aspect of
    the kingship of David, of whom it is said:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and his throne as the days of heaven&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 89:30]</span>, and as it is written:
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and his throne shall be established in kindness, and
    one who sits upon it in truth in the tent of David, judging and seeking
    judgment&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 16:5]</span>. And it is
    written of him: <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;and he shall not judge by the sight of
    his eyes&rdquo;</em></span>, etc., and <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;he shall judge
    the poor with righteousness&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yeshayahu 11:3&ndash;4]</span>. For the primary sprouting of
    the horn of Mashiach is through the rectification of <span class="key-term">mishpat</span>
    &mdash; through which Yerushalayim the Holy City shall be built, which is the aspect of
    the <span class="key-term">Haichal ha-Kodesh</span>, as above. And therefore all these
    blessings are juxtaposed to one another.
  </p>

